"page-layout usage" is a notebook page
	<header level="1">
		Page layout
		<header>A page layout puts what stays on screen around a page:  a menu across the top, a drawer of side navigation down the start, side bars either side of the page, and a footer.</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Put the regions around a page in its slots, and the page itself in no slot.

			- `menu`:  the app's top bar, a `<ui-app-menu>`, with a `<ui-site-nav-button>` and `<ui-site-breadcrumbs>` at its start.
			- `page-menu`:  a row of its own under it, usually `<ui-site-tabs>`.
			- `drawer`:  the side navigation, a `<ui-site-nav>`, a column the whole height at the start.
			- `left`, `right`:  side bars either side of the page;  `footer`:  across the bottom.
			- A region with nothing in it takes no room at all.
			- `drawer="compact"` shows only the drawer's icons:  the layout sets `collapsed` on each `<ui-site-nav>` in it.
			- `toggleDrawer()` is what a `<ui-site-nav-button>` calls:
			  - narrow, it slides the drawer in or out
			  - wide, it switches `drawer` and sends `ui-toggle`, `{ newState, oldState }`, each `compact` or `full`;
			    to `full` a frame later, so the column widens from where it was
			- The layout remembers nothing:  keep the choice yourself, and set `drawer` again next time.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			const layout = document.querySelector("ui-page-layout")
			layout.addEventListener("ui-toggle", (event) => {
			  localStorage.setItem("drawer", event.detail.newState)
			})
			if (localStorage.getItem("drawer") === "compact") layout.setAttribute("drawer", "compact")
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="In a Spell site">
		<markdown>
			A Spell site draws its page layout once, and puts each page in it:
			so going between pages redraws only the page, and the top bar, the drawer's open folders and the side bars
			stay as they are.

			- Its main layout is a page layout, in a file of its own;  each slot it fills is a drawing.
			- The drawer holds the site's navigation, and the page menu its tabs, unless the layout draws them.
			- A page picks another layout on its own line (`, in the bare layout`), or none (`, no layout`).
			- The site remembers each person's choice of a compact drawer, per site and layout.

			```spell
			main layout is a page layout
				to draw its app menu
					<app-menu>
						<site-nav-button slot="start" />
						<site-breadcrumbs slot="start" />
					</app-menu>
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			Escape closes the drawer open over the page.

			- The keyboard's focus coming into a compact drawer opens it at once, and closes it as it leaves.
			- Inside, the side navigation's own keys walk its pages.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			It adds no landmark of its own, as it's drawn inside another page (Run draws a site inside the editor):
			the side navigation is a `nav`, and the page's own content brings the rest.
			Its own menu button says `aria-expanded`, and so does a `<ui-site-nav-button>`.
		</>
	</>
